From: Wendy Labs <engineering@wendy.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] policy: retry saved Wi-Fi profiles after no-secrets failures

A transient WPA handshake failure can make the supplicant request a new
password. Without a secret agent, activation fails with NO_SECRETS even
when the persistent profile still contains the correct password. The
policy's no-secrets block then prevents later autoconnect attempts,
including after the ordinary retry cooldown.

For infrastructure WPA-PSK profiles with a nonempty, system-owned saved
key, count this as an ordinary autoconnect failure instead. Keep the
reported NO_SECRETS error and all upstream authentication behavior.
Normal retry limits, cooldown, user disconnect and profile deletion still
apply. No prior-success timestamp is required because OTA can replace
the timestamp database while preserving profiles.

This does not validate the password: a retained profile with a wrong key
will also retry later. Interactive callers can delete failed new profiles.
Other security types and missing, agent-owned or unsaved keys retain the
upstream no-secrets block.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [WendyOS unattended saved-profile retry policy]
Signed-off-by: Wendy Labs <engineering@wendy.dev>
---
--- a/src/core/nm-policy.c
+++ b/src/core/nm-policy.c
@@ -2217,6 +2217,37 @@
     return success;
 }

+static gboolean
+has_saved_wifi_psk(NMDevice *device, NMSettingsConnection *sett_conn)
+{
+    NMConnection              *connection;
+    NMSettingWireless         *s_wifi;
+    NMSettingWirelessSecurity *s_wsec;
+    const char                *mode;
+    const char                *psk;
+
+    if (nm_device_get_device_type(device) != NM_DEVICE_TYPE_WIFI)
+        return FALSE;
+
+    connection = nm_settings_connection_get_connection(sett_conn);
+    s_wifi     = nm_connection_get_setting_wireless(connection);
+    s_wsec     = nm_connection_get_setting_wireless_security(connection);
+    if (!s_wifi || !s_wsec)
+        return FALSE;
+
+    mode = nm_setting_wireless_get_mode(s_wifi);
+    if (mode && !nm_streq(mode, NM_SETTING_WIRELESS_MODE_INFRA))
+        return FALSE;
+    if (!nm_streq0(nm_setting_wireless_security_get_key_mgmt(s_wsec), "wpa-psk")
+        || nm_setting_wireless_security_get_psk_flags(s_wsec) != NM_SETTING_SECRET_FLAG_NONE)
+        return FALSE;
+
+    /* Authentication may have cleared the applied connection's secrets while
+     * asking for a replacement. The persistent profile still has our key. */
+    psk = nm_setting_wireless_security_get_psk(s_wsec);
+    return psk && psk[0];
+}
+
 static void
 device_state_changed(NMDevice           *device,
                      NMDeviceState       new_state,
@@ -2267,6 +2298,13 @@

             switch (nm_device_state_reason_check(reason)) {
             case NM_DEVICE_STATE_REASON_NO_SECRETS:
+                /* On unattended Wi-Fi, a failed request for a replacement key
+                 * does not mean the saved key is absent or wrong. Keep the
+                 * failure reason, but use normal retry accounting and cooldown
+                 * instead of blocking this profile until an agent intervenes. */
+                if (has_saved_wifi_psk(device, sett_conn))
+                    break;
+
                 /* we want to block the connection from auto-connect if it failed due to no-secrets.
                  * However, if a secret-agent registered, since the connection made the last
                  * secret-request, we do not block it. The new secret-agent might not yet
